{{also|Härö|härö|Haro|háro|hāro}}
==English==

===Etymology===
See [[harrow#Etymology 2|harrow]] or [[haro#French]].

The claim has been made that the word is from {{der|en|fro|haro}}, {{m|fro|harou}}, from two words, "Ha" and "Rollo", referring to Duke [[w:Rollo of Normandy|Rollo of Normandy]]; his name became well known as a cry for justice and was later shortened to "Haro."
-"The Little Duke", Charlotte Young, 1955

===Interjection===
{{en-intj}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} {{n-g|An exclamation of [[distress]]}}; [[alas]].
# {{lb|en|Channel Islands}} {{n-g|A call for help, a [[demand]] for [[protection]] against [[harm]], or for [[assistance]] to [[arrest]] an [[adversary]].}}

